---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { DIMENSIONS, VERDICTS } from '../lib/dimensions';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
---

<Base title="Scorecard" active="scorecard" description="The six weighted criteria used to score every company, what each score means and how verdicts are assigned.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Scorecard</p>
  <h1>Six criteria, explicit weights</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every company is scored 1 to 5 on the same six dimensions. The weights reflect what predicts success at pre-seed, where the team and
    the insight matter more than traction that does not exist yet. You can change the weights on the
    <a href={url('/#scoreboard')}>scoreboard</a> and see how the ranking responds.
  </p>

  <h2>Default weights</h2>
  <div class="card">
    {
      DIMENSIONS.map((d) => (
        <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(150px,230px) 1fr 48px;gap:14px;align-items:center;margin-bottom:10px">
          <span><strong>{d.label}</strong></span>
          <span class="bar"><i style={`width:${d.weight * 3.6}%`} /></span>
          <span class="num"><strong>{d.weight}%</strong></span>
        </div>
      ))
    }
    <p class="small muted" style="margin:10px 0 0">
      For deep-tech and biotech I shift weight from evidence toward technology and team. For software and services I shift toward
      evidence and unit economics. Any adjustment is stated on the company page.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What each score means</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>The question</th><th>A score of 1 looks like</th><th>A score of 5 looks like</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          DIMENSIONS.map((d) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{d.label}</strong><br /><span class="muted small">{d.weight}%</span></td>
              <td>{d.asks}</td>
              <td>{d.low}</td>
              <td>{d.high}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">A 3 is a credible company with no standout strength or weakness on that dimension. Half points are used where evidence is mixed.</p>

  <h2>From score to verdict</h2>
  <p class="muted">The score informs the verdict but does not set it. A company can score well and still be a pass if the return maths cannot work, so each verdict is a judgement with stated reasons.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Verdict</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          VERDICTS.map((v) => (
            <tr>
              <td><span class={`pill pill-${v.slug}`}>{v.id}</span></td>
              <td>{v.meaning}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">Every verdict lists what must be true for the investment to work and what would change the view.</p>

  <h2>The return maths behind it</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p style="margin:0">
      Illustrative, not a forecast. A 100M euro fund wants to return at least 3x, so 300M euros. A pre-seed cheque buys about 15% of a
      company, and after three or four later rounds that is diluted to roughly 7 to 8%. At 7.5% ownership, returning the whole fund from
      one company needs an exit near 1.3 billion euros. So each thesis has to show a credible path to a billion-euro outcome, or to a
      smaller exit at a very low entry price.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
